.TH "IDBOBJECTSTORE.GETALLRECORDS" "3JS" "July 6, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
IDBObjectStore.getAllRecords \- IDBObjectStore: getAllRecords() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]getAllRecords()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R] interface retrieves all records (including
primary keys and values) from the object store.
.PP
\f[CR]getAllRecords()\f[R] effectively combines the functionality of
\f[CR]getAllKeys()\f[R] and \f[CR]getAll()\f[R] by enumerating both
primary keys and values at the same time.
This combined operation enables certain data retrieval patterns to be
significantly faster than alternatives such as iteration with cursors.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getAllRecords()
getAllRecords(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
An options object whose properties can include:
.TP
\f[B]query\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A key or an \f[CR]IDBKeyRange\f[R] identifying the records to retrieve.
If this value is \f[CR]null\f[R] or not specified, the browser will use
an unbound key range.
.TP
\f[B]count\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
The number of records to return.
If this value exceeds the number of records in the query, the browser
will retrieve only the queried records.
If the value is less than \f[CR]0\f[R] or greater than
\f[CR]2\(ha32 \- 1\f[R], a \f[CR]TypeError\f[R] exception will be
thrown.
.TP
\f[B]direction\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An enumerated value specifying the direction in which the records are
traversed, which in turn defines the order in which they are returned.
Possible values are:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]next\f[R]
The records are traversed from the beginning, in increasing key order.
This is the default value.
.TP
\f[B]nextunique\f[R]
The records are traversed from the beginning, in increasing key order.
This will yield the same records as \f[CR]next\f[R], because duplicate
keys are not allowed in \f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R]s.
.TP
\f[B]prev\f[R]
The records are traversed from the end, in decreasing key order.
.TP
\f[B]prevunique\f[R]
The records are traversed from the end, in decreasing key order.
This will yield the same records as \f[CR]prev\f[R], because duplicate
keys are not allowed in \f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R]s.
.RE
.SS Return value
An \f[CR]IDBRequest\f[R] object on which subsequent events related to
this operation are fired.
.PP
If the operation is successful, the value of the request\(cqs
\f[CR]result\f[R] property is an \f[CR]array\f[R] of
\f[CR]IDBRecord\f[R] instances representing all records that match the
given query, up to the number specified by \f[CR]count\f[R] (if
provided).
.PP
Each \f[CR]IDBRecord\f[R] instance contains the following properties:
.TP
\f[B]key\f[R]
A value representing the record\(cqs key.
This is identical to the \f[CR]primaryKey\f[R] property.
.TP
\f[B]primaryKey\f[R]
The record\(cqs primary key.
.TP
\f[B]value\f[R]
A value representing the record\(cqs value.
.SS Exceptions
This method may raise a \f[CR]DOMException\f[R] of the following types:
.TP
\f[B]InvalidStateError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the \f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R] has been deleted or removed.
.TP
\f[B]TransactionInactiveError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if this \f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R]\(cqs transaction is inactive.
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the \f[CR]count\f[R] parameter is not between \f[CR]0\f[R] and
\f[CR]2\(ha32 \- 1\f[R], inclusive.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
This example queries an \f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R] for up to 100 records
whose keys come after \f[CR]\(dqmyKey\(dq\f[R], with results sorted in
reverse order.
.PP
The code first creates a transaction on an \f[CR]IDBDatabase\f[R] named
\f[CR]db\f[R] (omitting the code to open the database), and then uses it
to get an \f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R] containing a contacts list.
It then calls \f[CR]getAllRecords()\f[R] on the object store, returning
a \f[CR]IDBRequest\f[R] instance.
Event listeners are added to this request for the \f[CR]success\f[R] and
\f[CR]error\f[R] events.
On success, the result \f[CR]event.target.result\f[R] is logged (this
also available as \f[CR]request.result\f[R]).
This result contains an array of \f[CR]IDBRecord\f[R] instances.
Note that because this is a query on an \f[CR]IDBObjectStore\f[R], the
\f[CR]key\f[R] and \f[CR]primaryKey\f[R] in each record have the same
value.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Create a transaction on the database and use it to get the contained store\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] transaction = db.transaction([\(dqcontactsList\(dq], \(dqreadonly\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] objectStore = transaction.objectStore(\(dqcontactsList\(dq);

\f[B]const\f[R] query = IDBKeyRange.lowerBound(\(dqmyKey\(dq, \f[B]true\f[R]);

\f[B]const\f[R] request = objectStore.getAllRecords({
  query,
  count: 100,
  direction: \(dqprev\(dq,
});

request.addEventListener(\(dqsuccess\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] myRecords = event.target.result; \f[I]// Array of IDBRecord instances\f[R]
  console.log(myRecords);
});

request.addEventListener(\(dqerror\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.error(\(dqError retrieving records:\(dq, event.target.error);
});
.EE
